# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# The repository has always stored LF-normalized blobs; what changed is the checkout side. The
# previous `eol=crlf` converted on checkout, which meant every tool that writes LF -- and most
# do -- left files disagreeing with the declared policy, and git warned about each one on every
# commit. Conversion is the thing generating the noise, so there is no conversion now.
#
# Nothing here needs CRLF: there are no .bat, .cmd or .ps1 files, and the .NET and Node
# toolchains read LF happily on Windows. `text=auto` still lets git detect and leave binaries
# alone. Being explicit also means the result no longer depends on each contributor's
# core.autocrlf, which is the point of committing a policy at all.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets — never apply line-ending conversion.
*.png    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.xlsx   binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.lockb  binary
